WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.552 --> 00:00:10.676
is an illness that has been known for thousands of years, and for most of history, people diagnosed

2
00:00:10.696 --> 00:00:13.378
with diabetes have suffered short and painful lives.

3
00:00:14.199 --> 00:00:18.883
After its symptoms appeared, the patient would invariably die, usually within a year.

4
00:00:19.824 --> 00:00:24.908
It causes blindness, nerve damage, and gangrene, which can lead to the loss of limbs.

5
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:32.183
Early physicians knew that sugar was somehow involved because patients with diabetes produced urine with a strange, sweet

6
00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:40.006
smell. For thousands of years, the suffering of diabetics went unchecked, until a small-town doctor in Canada thought

7
00:00:40.026 --> 00:00:41.967
of a new approach to studying the disease.

8
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:47.349
Dr. Frederick Banting was born on a farm near Alliston, Ontario.

9
00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:52.971
In 1920, he had a small medical practice in London, Ontario, but was teaching classes to earn some

10
00:00:53.011 --> 00:00:54.032
supplementary income.

11
00:00:55.116 --> 00:00:58.378
Doctors of his time knew that the pancreas was linked to diabetes.

12
00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:04.422
Experiments had shown that dogs whose pancreases had been surgically removed died from diabetes.

13
00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:10.085
They knew that something essential must be present in the pancreas, but finding it was frustrating.

14
00:01:10.946 --> 00:01:16.309
A healthy pancreas, it was believed, released a substance to deal with the sugar our bodies absorb when

15
00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:16.729
we eat.

16
00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.537
One evening, when preparing a lecture for his class, it occurred to Banting that the pancreas of a

17
00:01:22.577 --> 00:01:26.200
diabetic might destroy this substance before it can be used by the body.

18
00:01:27.121 --> 00:01:30.103
At two o'clock in the morning, he jotted down a quick note.

19
00:01:30.984 --> 00:01:34.126
It read, "Ligate pancreatic ducts of dogs."

20
00:01:34.967 --> 00:01:40.872
Banting's idea was to close off the small passages in a dog's pancreas that release its products into

21
00:01:40.912 --> 00:01:41.892
the rest of its body.

22
00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:44.675
His idea led to the discovery of insulin.

23
00:01:45.756 --> 00:01:49.238
Banting took his theory to the University of Toronto's physiology department.

24
00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:50.379
John R.

25
00:01:51.212 --> 00:01:58.118
Macleod, the head of the department, was skeptical of Banting's ideas, but nevertheless gave Banting laboratory space, ten

26
00:01:58.158 --> 00:02:02.582
laboratory dogs, and an assistant, a medical student named Charles Best.

27
00:02:03.643 --> 00:02:08.287
Banting tested his idea by performing surgery on some of the dogs to tie off the ducts in

28
00:02:08.307 --> 00:02:08.967
the pancreas.

29
00:02:09.828 --> 00:02:13.391
That way, any insulin they produced would remain inside the organ.

30
00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:19.076
After several weeks, Banting removed the pancreases and ground them in a solution of salt water.

31
00:02:20.232 --> 00:02:25.417
When this solution was injected into a diabetic dog, its symptoms improved almost instantaneously.

32
00:02:26.778 --> 00:02:32.923
Our bodies get energy by converting the carbohydrates in sweet or starchy foods into a particular sugar called

33
00:02:32.963 --> 00:02:39.649
glucose. But despite hours between meals and the occasional dessert loaded with sugar, the level of glucose in

34
00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:41.470
our blood stays relatively constant.

35
00:02:42.691 --> 00:02:48.016
It is insulin, a hormone produced in the pancreas, that regulates the amount of glucose in the blood.

36
00:02:48.876 --> 00:02:53.779
But diabetics either don't produce insulin or their bodies do not react to the insulin in their blood

37
00:02:53.899 --> 00:03:00.702
correctly. In a healthy person, insulin removes the excess blood sugar by storing it in the kidneys, as

38
00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:05.225
well as in muscle and fat cells, from where it can be released as needed.

39
00:03:06.025 --> 00:03:11.869
When a diabetic consumes food, the amount of sugar in the blood rises to toxic levels, damaging the

40
00:03:11.909 --> 00:03:12.689
body's tissues.

41
00:03:13.409 --> 00:03:18.012
The insulin Banting injected into the diabetic dog was all it needed to improve its health.

42
00:03:18.732 --> 00:03:20.974
The result was confirmed in other dogs.

43
00:03:22.115 --> 00:03:27.439
After this success, Macleod decided he would turn his attention to the project as well, and he brought

44
00:03:27.499 --> 00:03:30.702
in an expert in biochemistry to help, James Collip.

45
00:03:31.743 --> 00:03:35.266
Now that a team of four was assembled, new discoveries came quickly.

46
00:03:35.946 --> 00:03:41.171
They found that it wasn't necessary to ligate a dog's pancreatic ducts in order to collect insulin.

47
00:03:42.051 --> 00:03:45.954
With the proper technique, insulin could be extracted from a healthy pancreas.

48
00:03:46.615 --> 00:03:51.927
With the help of Collip, the team was able to purify the insulin they extracted.

49
00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:57.912
In 1922, their insulin extract was tried for the first time on a human patient, a 14-year-old boy

50
00:03:57.952 --> 00:03:58.953
named Leonard Thompson.

51
00:03:59.754 --> 00:04:04.177
Leonard was desperately sick with diabetes, but the treatment had a remarkable effect.

52
00:04:05.118 --> 00:04:08.161
His symptoms improved, but it was too early to celebrate.

53
00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:12.564
Leonard Thompson suffered a severe allergic reaction to the insulin extract.

54
00:04:13.665 --> 00:04:19.385
Although Banting and his colleagues had treated the boy's diabetes, they had also exposed him to unwanted chemicals.

55
00:04:20.386 --> 00:04:25.729
While Banting and Best searched for a better source of insulin, Macleod and Collip worked on its purification.

56
00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.252
Banting and Best had demonstrated that diabetes could be successfully treated.

57
00:04:31.352 --> 00:04:36.715
Turning a laboratory experiment into a treatment for an illness found all over the globe, however, was another

58
00:04:36.755 --> 00:04:37.596
matter entirely.

59
00:04:38.596 --> 00:04:40.477
One problem was collecting enough insulin.

60
00:04:41.138 --> 00:04:44.840
Cows and pigs were being slaughtered by the thousands in Toronto's abattoirs.

61
00:04:45.644 --> 00:04:48.646
Banting thought they might be a good and abundant source of the material.

62
00:04:49.606 --> 00:04:55.330
Experiments on cows and pigs were successful, meaning that both animals could be sources of insulin for diabetics.

63
00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:00.693
Banting and Best worked very hard to improve their formula, but they were both aware that while they

64
00:05:00.713 --> 00:05:03.975
were studying the problem, diabetics around the world were dying.

65
00:05:04.635 --> 00:05:10.459
Both realized that only a large pharmaceutical company had the resources needed to produce insulin extract in massive

66
00:05:10.499 --> 00:05:12.400
quantities and quickly purify it.

67
00:05:13.728 --> 00:05:18.510
Banting wanted insulin to be affordable and available to everyone who needed it, so he sold the commercial

68
00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:21.792
rights to insulin to the University of Toronto for a single dollar.

69
00:05:22.612 --> 00:05:29.315
The university then negotiated a deal with Eli Lilly and Company, an American pharmaceutical company, to mass-produce insulin

70
00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:30.175
in North America.

71
00:05:31.016 --> 00:05:35.558
Within one year of its discovery, insulin went from a laboratory experiment to a medicine.

72
00:05:36.378 --> 00:05:38.299
Insulin is not a cure for diabetes.

73
00:05:39.179 --> 00:05:40.880
It is, however, an effective treatment.

74
00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:47.382
With regular injections and a carefully managed diet, diabetics now look forward to longer, healthier lives.

75
00:05:48.242 --> 00:05:53.963
All four men played significant parts in creating a treatment for diabetes, but when the discovery of insulin

76
00:05:54.003 --> 00:05:57.424
was first reported, newspapers credited Macleod alone.

77
00:05:58.205 --> 00:06:03.426
Although Macleod corrected them and explained Banting's role, the event led to discord between the two men.

78
00:06:04.406 --> 00:06:10.268
When the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was announced in 1923, Macleod and Banting were honored.

79
00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:16.918
Banting shared his prize money with Best, whom he considered his real partner, and Macleod shared his with

80
00:06:16.958 --> 00:06:20.020
Collip. Later, in 1934, Dr.

81
00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.701
Banting was knighted and became Sir Frederick Banting.

82
00:06:23.461 --> 00:06:27.363
For many years, the insulin extract used to treat diabetics came from animals.

83
00:06:28.103 --> 00:06:33.525
Some people, however, are allergic to the insulin that animals produce because it is slightly different from that

84
00:06:33.585 --> 00:06:34.505
produced by humans.

85
00:06:35.326 --> 00:06:40.996
With the advent of genetic engineering, it has become possible to change the DNA of small bacteria so

86
00:06:41.036 --> 00:06:43.638
that they produce insulin identical to that of humans.

87
00:06:44.538 --> 00:06:49.902
Today, it is estimated that over 175 million people worldwide have diabetes.

88
00:06:50.682 --> 00:06:56.526
Thanks to the work of a small-town doctor in Ontario, Canada, diabetes is no longer a death sentence.

89
00:06:57.366 --> 00:07:01.348
Now, people diagnosed with this disease can live long and healthy lives.
